WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.800
Storage location matters.

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:08.980
Sensitive uploads should often be stored outside the web root or in private object storage so they cannot be fetched directly by guessing a path.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.720
Public storage should be reserved for files intentionally meant to be public.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:19.340
Even then, publication should be a deliberate step, not the default result of receiving a file from a user or integration.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:25.240
Separating upload, processing, quarantine, and published areas reduces accidental exposure.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.460
An uploaded file can land in an intake area with limited permissions.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.680
A scanner or processor can move accepted files into a processing area.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.380
Files awaiting review can remain quarantined.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.200
Published files can be placed in an area designed for controlled download.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.740
This separation lets permissions and monitoring match the file's current state.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.440
Least-privilege permissions are essential.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:52.860
The web application may need write access to an upload area, but it may not need execute permissions.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:01:01.240
A processing worker may need read access to intake files and write access to output files, but not broad access to every tenant's storage.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:09.940
Cloud object storage policies should clearly distinguish private buckets, public buckets, service identities, and human administrative access.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.080
Tenant separation and access control should be built into storage design.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.980
Do not let a client-supplied path decide where an object lands.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.800
Do not rely on predictable file names as the access control model.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:35.380
Store server-controlled identifiers, ownership metadata, tenant context, classification, processing state, and retention information so that every later download, share, or delete operation can be authorized against trusted data.
